WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.239
Here's a step condition: run only on the main branch.

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:07.845
The run is on main.

3
00:00:07.845 --> 00:00:11.192
So the condition is true, and the step runs.

4
00:00:11.192 --> 00:00:11.648
Right?

5
00:00:11.648 --> 00:00:12.485
Not always.

6
00:00:12.485 --> 00:00:15.756
And the reason is a rule nobody wrote down.

7
00:00:16.533 --> 00:00:19.191
Picture the closing routine in our kitchen.

8
00:00:19.191 --> 00:00:23.395
A note on the wall says: on Saturdays, the last cook mops the floor.

9
00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:24.652
It's Saturday.

10
00:00:24.652 --> 00:00:26.491
The evening runs along.

11
00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:29.489
At nine o'clock, a pot of oil tips over.

12
00:00:29.667 --> 00:00:33.258
The house rule says everyone stops at the first problem.

13
00:00:33.258 --> 00:00:36.016
So the evening ends early, and nobody mops.

14
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.895
It was the one night the floor really needed it.

15
00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:40.369
The note was clear.

16
00:00:40.369 --> 00:00:43.112
Its condition was true: it was Saturday.

17
00:00:43.267 --> 00:00:46.572
What nobody read was the unwritten rule underneath.

18
00:00:46.572 --> 00:00:49.424
And only if everything has gone well so far.

19
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:55.751
Workflow conditions carry the same invisible rule, written, if you like, in invisible ink.

20
00:00:56.667 --> 00:00:58.546
Now the same story in a job.

21
00:00:58.546 --> 00:01:02.440
A job is a list of steps that run in order on one machine.

22
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.916
The third step has our note: upload the report, but only on main.

23
00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.618
The run is on main.

24
00:01:08.618 --> 00:01:09.924
Checkout passes.

25
00:01:09.924 --> 00:01:11.394
Then a test fails.

26
00:01:11.394 --> 00:01:12.619
That's the oil.

27
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.315
The condition is true.

28
00:01:14.315 --> 00:01:17.208
Pause and guess: does the upload step run?

29
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:19.696
No.

30
00:01:19.696 --> 00:01:20.977
It's skipped.

31
00:01:20.977 --> 00:01:23.047
Exactly like the mop.

32
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:23.932
Here's why.

33
00:01:23.932 --> 00:01:30.253
Every if that doesn't mention a status function gets an invisible success() added to the front.

34
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.405
success() asks one question: did everything before this succeed?

35
00:01:34.405 --> 00:01:36.908
After the failed test, the answer is no.

36
00:01:36.908 --> 00:01:38.911
So the whole condition is false.

37
00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:42.206
A step with no if at all behaves the same way.

38
00:01:42.206 --> 00:01:45.209
That's how a job stops at its first failure.

39
00:01:45.209 --> 00:01:47.870
Every later step asks, so far, so good?

40
00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:48.757
And hears no.

41
00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:52.260
Honestly, success() could have been named so far so good.

42
00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:53.427
That's all it means.

43
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:56.789
Now that you've met one, here's the family.

44
00:01:56.789 --> 00:02:01.472
They're called status functions: questions about what happened earlier in the run.

45
00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:04.315
success(): everything before succeeded.

46
00:02:04.315 --> 00:02:05.605
That's the default.

47
00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:08.004
failure(): something before failed.

48
00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.340
cancelled(): the run was cancelled.

49
00:02:10.533 --> 00:02:14.849
And always(): true no matter what, even while the run is being cancelled.

50
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:21.343
So when you mean something other than so far so good, you say so, by writing the status function yourself.

51
00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:23.533
Back to the report.

52
00:02:23.533 --> 00:02:27.263
A test report is most useful exactly when a test failed.

53
00:02:27.263 --> 00:02:32.527
So write !cancelled(): run whatever happened, unless someone cancelled the run.

54
00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:35.478
Now the upload runs after the failed test.

55
00:02:35.478 --> 00:02:39.763
That's the condition the Day 25 pipeline put on its upload step.

56
00:02:39.933 --> 00:02:46.788
And to run only when one particular step failed, combine them: failure(), and that step's outcome is failure.

57
00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:49.776
The same rule works between jobs.

58
00:02:49.776 --> 00:02:54.763
In the lesson's practice workflow, build needs version, and report needs both.

59
00:02:54.933 --> 00:03:00.807
The report job only reads and prints the other jobs' results, so it carries if: always().

60
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.865
Now tick the box that breaks the build on purpose.

61
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.039
Version passes.

62
00:03:05.039 --> 00:03:06.354
Build exits with 1.

63
00:03:06.354 --> 00:03:12.724
And report still runs, thanks to always(), and prints what it saw: one success, one failure.

64
00:03:12.867 --> 00:03:19.871
Without always(), report would have been skipped, quietly, and the one job that tells you what went wrong would say nothing.

65
00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.129
So should we sprinkle always() everywhere, to be safe?

66
00:03:24.129 --> 00:03:24.931
Please don't.

67
00:03:25.067 --> 00:03:28.856
An always() step also runs while the run is being cancelled.

68
00:03:28.856 --> 00:03:31.698
If it gets stuck, it hangs until the timeout.

69
00:03:31.698 --> 00:03:34.793
GitHub's docs warn against it on important steps.

70
00:03:34.933 --> 00:03:38.007
To run whatever happened, prefer !cancelled().

71
00:03:38.007 --> 00:03:41.816
Keep always() for small jobs that only report, like ours.

72
00:03:42.733 --> 00:03:43.919
So here's the moral.

73
00:03:43.919 --> 00:03:45.876
Every if gets a hidden success().

74
00:03:45.876 --> 00:03:49.729
When you mean something else, write the status function yourself.

75
00:03:49.867 --> 00:03:52.903
And the floor gets mopped, whatever happened at nine.

76
00:03:53.067 --> 00:03:53.979
Quick check.

77
00:03:53.979 --> 00:03:55.957
A step says if: failure().

78
00:03:55.957 --> 00:03:58.543
An earlier step in the job failed.

79
00:03:58.543 --> 00:03:59.456
Does it run?

80
00:03:59.456 --> 00:04:00.673
Pause and think.

81
00:04:02.867 --> 00:04:03.150
Yes.

82
00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:09.024
It names a status function, so no hidden success() is added, and failure() is true.
